{
  "dom_id": "step-q28208",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14216079/assets/7d341093cf35425a75174a6871d452c38daa0d281cdc2a2b7aaceeca390dee5b.png)\n\nWhich of the following curves is the graph of $y={x}^{3}?$",
  "worked_solution": "First, remember that for $x>0,$ the graph of $y={x}^{3}$ increases very rapidly as we increase $x.$ We say that $y\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$ The only graphs that exhibit this increasing property are II and IV.\n\nNow, we need to choose between graphs II and IV. To do this, let's evaluate $y={x}^{3}$ at some value of $x,$ say $x=-1.$ Substituting $x=-1$ into the function, we get\n\n$$\ny=(-1)^{3}=-1,\n$$\n\nso the correct graph must pass through the point $(-1,-1).$ Only graph IV satisfies this criterion.\n\nTherefore, graph IV corresponds to the graph of $y={x}^{3}.$",
